          %h2#idea Idea
          %p Universal properties are commonly used in mathematics, often without mentioning the term “universal property”.
          %p
            For example, if one were asked to give a map
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{C}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi i
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \mapsto (x^2, x + i x)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{C}
            is given, namely
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ↦
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi i
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \mapsto x^2, x \mapsto x + i x)
            %strong universal property
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            says that giving a map to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times B
            is the same as giving a map to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and a map to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
            Similarly, given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+homomorphism"} ring homomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \to S
            to a homomorphism from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+ring"} polynomial ring
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R[x] \to S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            that we send
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            to. In other words, a homomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R[x] \to S
            is the same as a homomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \to S
            and an element of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            For it to be a universal property, just the existence of such a bijection is not sufficient. We will need some conditions to make sure the bijection is “natural”. Abstractly, this says that the bijection is given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
            of certain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            %a{:href => "#ConcreteExample"} Concrete examples
            for more details.
          %p
            Recall that by the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            %em definitions
            of the constructions! These are known as
            = succeed "." do
              %strong universal constructions
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor+theorem"} adjoint functor theorem
          %h2#example Example
          %h3#ConcreteExample Concrete examples
          %p We first look at a few concrete examples of universal properties. These are all special cases of the ones described below.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_products Example (products)
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
              of two objects (eg. sets, groups, rings etc.) is specified by the property that maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to A \times B
              biject naturally with pairs of maps
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 1
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 2
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f_1: X \to A, f_2: X \to B)
            %p
              The naturality condition is that if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to A \times B
              corresponds to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 1
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 2
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1: X \to A, f_2: X \to B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: Y \to X
              is a map, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \circ g
              corresponds to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1 \circ g
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_2 \circ g
            %p
              In particular, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+map"} identity map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi id
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id: A \times B \to A \times B
              corresponds to a pair of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection+maps"} projection maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1: A \times B \to A
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 2
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2: A \times B \to B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to A \times B
              is a map, then by naturality, it corresponds to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1 \circ f: X \to A
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2 \circ f: X \to B
            %p
              Suppose we are not given a bijection, but just an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              with maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1: P \to A
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 2
                      %mo :
                      %mi P
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2: P \to B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to P
              to pairs of maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to A, X \to B
              by composition. This makes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              into the product of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              exactly when this function is a bijection, ie. for any pair of maps
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 1
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 2
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1 : X \to A, f_2: X \to B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to P
              whose compositions with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1, \pi_2
              are
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1, f_2
              respectively (naturality is easy to check).
            %p
              (The experienced reader will notice that this is just a special case of the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            %p
              Thus, the universal property can be stated as follows:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a product of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              if there exists maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1: C \to A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2: C \to B
              such that given any pair of maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1: X \to A
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 2
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_2: X \to B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to C
              such that the following diagram commutes:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi f
                                    %mn 1
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ↘
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi f
                                    %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ←
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi π
                                  %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi π
                                  %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp; &amp; X &amp; &amp; \\
                    \&amp; ^\mathllap{f_1}\swarrow &amp; \downarrow^\mathrlap{f} &amp; \searrow^\mathrlap{f_2}\\
                    A &amp; \underset{\pi_1}{\leftarrow} &amp; C &amp; \underset{\pi_2}{\rightarrow} &amp; B
                    }
            %p
              In this case, we tend to write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times B
              for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            Note that if we are talking about sets, then an element of a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is equivalent to a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to X
            from the singleton set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times B
            is the same as a pair of elements
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a, b)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in B
          .num_example
            %h6#example_free_groups Example (free groups)
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+group"} free
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              generators is a group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n
              such that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+homomorphisms"} group homomorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n \to G
              bijects (naturally) with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              elements of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              (not necessarily distinct).
            %p
              Similar to the above, the naturality condition says if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: F_n \to G
              corresponds to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_1, ..., g_n \in G
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h: G \to H
              is a map, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \circ f
              corresponds to the elements
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo ,
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(g_1), ..., h(g_n)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi id
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id: F_n \to F_n
              corresponds to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              elements
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1, ..., x_n\in F_n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: F_n \to G
              corresponds to the elements
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x_1), ..., f(x_n)
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %p
              Thus, given the specified elements
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1, ..., x_n \in F_n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              elements of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: F_n \to G
              that sends
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1, ..., x_n
              to the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              elements.
            %p
              Diagrammatically, picking
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              elements out of a set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is the same as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              (of sets)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \to X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(G)
              for the underlying set of the group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              (ie.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
              to
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo :
                      %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: n \to U(F_n)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: n \to U(G)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{f}: F_n \to G
              such that the following diagram commutes:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi F
                              %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi U
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mover
                                  %mi f
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi G
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↗
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi f
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    U(F_n) &amp; \overset{U(\tilde{f})}{\to} &amp; U(G)\\
                    ^\mathllap{\phi}\uparrow &amp; \nearrow_{\mathrlap{f}}\\
                    n
                    }
            %p
              In other words, every map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: n \to U(G)
              factors through the universal map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: n \to U(F_n)
              uniquely.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_tensor_products Example (tensor products)
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
              has the universal property that a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bilinear+map"} bilinear map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ×
                    %mi W
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \times W \to U
              bijects naturally with linear maps
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi W
                      %mo →
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \otimes W \to U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi V
                    %mo ×
                    %mi W
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: V \times W \to V \otimes W
              such that every bilinear map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ×
                    %mi W
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \times W \to U
              factors through
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              uniquely.
          %p We have more degenerate examples such as the terminal object:
          .num_example
            %h6#example_terminal_and_initial_objects Example (terminal and initial objects)
            %p
              In the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              satisfies the property that there is always a unique map from any object to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to 1
              biject (necessarily naturally) with the set
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is such that there is always a unique map from any object to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is called the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            %p
              Dually, an initial object is an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              such that there is a unique map from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              to any object
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %h3#classes_of_examples Classes of examples
          %p
            In general, the
            %strong universal constructions
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable functor
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functor
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"}> limit
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/end"}> end
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extension"} Kan extension
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"}> dependent sum
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
          %p
            Each of these may be defined by requiring it to satisfy a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong universal property
          %p In good cases, every single one of these is a special case of every other, so somehow one single concept here comes to us with many different faces.
          %p
            Some or all of these have analogs in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            theory and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-limit"} 2-limit
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-adjunction"} 2-adjunction
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+in+a+quasi-category"} limit in a quasi-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} adjoint (∞,1)-functor
          %h2#references References
          %p
            An introductory but thorough treatment of universal properties and their relationship with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            is given in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"}> Emily Riehl
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Category Theory in context
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://emilyriehl.github.io/files/context.pdf"} pdf
          %p A more informal and intuitive account is given in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paolo+Perrone"}> Paolo Perrone
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Notes on Category Theory with examples from basic mathematics
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1912.10642"} arXiv
